TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "defensive effect on opponent second-half scoring change",
    "H2-H1 differential as a team-level metric",
    "reliability of team defensive trait",
    "half-time adjustment consistency",
    "year-over-year correlation of opponent second-half adjustment"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to isolate the defensive team's causal effect from opponent coaching, fatigue, and game script?",
    "What is the within-team game-to-game variance of opponent H2-H1 change, and how many games are needed for reliable estimation?",
    "Does the metric exhibit year-over-year stability for the same team after controlling for roster and coaching changes?",
    "Can regression-to-the-mean artifacts be separated from true halftime adjustment effects?",
    "How much between-team variance exists relative to within-team variance?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No prior studies exist to indicate whether the effect exists or is measurable, what magnitude to expect, or which confounders dominate."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question requires quantifying a causal, within-game effect aggregated to the team level and then assessing its temporal stability. This is conceptually well-defined but statistically demanding.",
    "Opponent H2-H1 change is influenced by many factors beyond the defense (opponent's own coaching, fatigue, score effects, rotation changes), making isolation difficult without strong assumptions.",
    "A reliability study would need to compute the metric for each team-season, account for confounders using pre-game and first-half-only covariates, and then assess stability via intraclass correlation or year-over-year correlation.",
    "Even with 120 team-seasons, statistical power to detect a small reliability signal may be limited because the primary source of variation is game-level noise, and the number of independent team-level observations per season is only 30."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Whether roster and coaching continuity across seasons can be sufficiently controlled to interpret year-over-year changes as the same trait.",
    "The extent to which garbage time and blowout dynamics distort H2-H1 differences.",
    "Whether a linear difference H2-H1 is the appropriate functional form; a ratio or per-possession metric might be more stable.",
    "The magnitude of true between-team variance, which is unknown and could be zero."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was retrieved, so the assessment relies entirely on reasoning from first principles, data description, and risk identification."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.55,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "With only 30 teams per season and high game-level variance in H2-H1 differences, the between-team signal may be too weak to yield reliable intraclass correlations or treat them as a stable trait, especially after adjusting for confounders."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "H2-H1 point differential is a noisy, bounded difference that may not capture the defensive adjustment construct reliably; measurement error inflates within-team variance and attenuates reliability estimates."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Opponent second-half performance is strongly influenced by factors unrelated to the defense (e.g., opponent's own halftime adjustments, fatigue, foul trouble, home-court advantage), which cannot be fully disentangled without random assignment of opponents to teams."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior project records were retrieved, so it is unknown whether this specific question has been addressed before within the project."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The effective sample size is 30 teams per season, and the ICC for a noisy within-game metric may be low, leading to imprecise estimates and low power to detect a non-zero reliability."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The primary outcome—opponent H2-H1 scoring change—is a difference of two noisy halves; as an endpoint it may have low signal-to-noise ratio and erratic season-to-season stability, undermining attempts to establish it as a reliable trait."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Confounders such as opponent quality, game location, rest days, in-game injuries, and score-based strategic shifts cannot be fully adjusted for with observational data, risking biased estimates of the defense-specific effect."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Teams with extreme first-half outcomes will naturally regress toward the mean in the second half, creating an illusory 'adjustment' effect that may masquerade as a defensive trait, especially if teams are selected on first-half performance."
    },
    {
      "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
      "text": "Scoring is bounded at zero; second-half change can be constrained by first-half score, creating ceiling/floor effects that distort correlations and reliability measures."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.15,
    "rejected": 0.25,
    "untestable": 0.15,
    "artifact": 0.35,
    "uncertain": 0.10
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The team-specific mean effect on opponent second-half points per 100 possessions after controlling for opponent first-half offensive rating, game location, and rest advantage. Reliability is the intraclass correlation (ICC(2,1)) of this team effect across games within a season, and the Pearson correlation of the shrunken team effect estimates between consecutive seasons.",
    "unit": "Team-season as the unit of reliability analysis; individual games as the unit of effect estimation.",
    "sample": "All regular-season games from 2022-23 through 2025-26 (4 full seasons), excluding games where the point differential at halftime exceeds 25 points (to reduce garbage-time distortion). 30 teams per season, total 120 team-seasons.",
    "power": "Assuming a true ICC of 0.10 and 30 teams with approximately 75 games each per season, the standard error of the ICC estimate is approximately 0.08 (based on Fisher transformation), yielding low power to distinguish it from zero. With four seasons, the effective team-level sample for year-over-year correlation becomes 90 pairs, providing roughly 60% power to detect a correlation of 0.2 at α=0.05.",
    "controls": "Fixed effects for opponent first-half offensive rating (to remove baseline defensive performance), game location (home/away), rest days differential, and indicator for back-to-back games. Stratify by season month to control for seasonal fatigue trends. All controls must be measurable prior to the second half or be fully exogenous.",
    "falsification": "1) Test whether the same team effect predicts opponent H2-H1 change in the first 10 games vs the last 10 games of a season within the same year; low split-half reliability would refute the stable-trait interpretation. 2) Replace opponent H2-H1 with a randomly assigned dummy outcome (shuffled team labels) to confirm the ICC falls to zero. 3) Check that the effect does not predict opponent's third-quarter change specifically (to rule out halftime-specific causality) more than second-half overall.",
    "multiplicity": "A single reliability metric (ICC) and a single year-over-year correlation are pre-specified as co-primary, tested at α=0.05 each with no correction for two tests; all other analyses are exploratory.",
    "decision_rule": "Proceed to claim the trait is reliable if the lower bound of the 95% confidence interval for the season-averaged ICC exceeds 0.05 AND the year-over-year correlation is positive with one-sided p < 0.10. Otherwise treat the hypothesis as unsupported.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular season and playoff play-by-play, rotation, and matchup data as available on 2026-10-06, excluding any partial-season data from 2025-26 prior to completion."
  }
}

